{"kind":"assert","text":"Report for the planner, as the 09:01 survey asked: the HTTP replacement failure under f49277c8 is a production defect, not a test race. The earlier late-abort hypothesis is false. Nothing has changed on main or on a Worker.\n\nDefect: McpHttp.handle passes only request.signal to #stream (packages/mcp/src/http.ts:231). Node's Request links its source signal through a WeakRef to an internal controller that the Request alone holds. Once the Request is collected, the abort no longer reaches request.signal. A client that left during the opening then still gets a stream: #stream installs it and closes the session's live stream.\n\nEffect: on a legacy stream of the Node server serveHttp, a replacement whose client has gone can end the session's live stream. No authority or stored data is involved. The hosted Worker runs http.ts on workerd's Request, which I have not tested.\n\nEvidence (retained in ~/play/artroom-evidence/2026-10-31-http-request-lifetime-diagnosis, SHA256SUMS digest 67cfbd79db664fa8bbdbcba00a326ecc5d70c6d12654d526b65a8305d98b6cc9; machine 18 CPUs, Node 26.13.0, counts from one shell loop per row):\n- Trace of a failing unchanged owner: the server's own controller aborted 1 ms after the client abort. R2's check read signal.aborted=false 108 ms later, and it installed a stream and closed B. So the test's 100 ms sleep was long enough.\n- Plain Node, after the Request is unreachable and collected: the signal followed the abort 0 of 20 times. With the Request kept, 20 of 20.\n- New witness at the real layer, with a forced collection: fails 3 of 3 on unchanged code (expected 200 to be 204), passes 3 of 3 with the Request kept reachable until the check.\n- Whole owner under the reproducing command: unchanged 5 of 24 failed, with the fix 0 of 24 failed.\n- CPU load does not produce it: 6 of 6 passed at load up to 47.\n- The H8 failure follows from the first: that case dies before its cleanup and its streams keep the slots.\n\nNot shown: no GC trace covers the failing server path, so collection there is inferred from the plain Node result, the witness and the fix. Why node.ts's await does not always keep the Request alive is also unproven; optimized code dropping the dead variable is my explanation, untested. The reproducing command sets a 256 descriptor limit and the JSON reporter, and I did not separate those two.\n\nConsequences: the fixture-only proposal V2 will not land. Its observer closure keeps each Request alive, so the test would pass with the defect still in place. Request 504c3d88 forbids a production change, so I am filing a separate request and head: keep the Request reachable until the check in #stream, plus the witness, with all nine original cases unchanged. It goes through the gate and checker review like any other head, lands on main without a deployment, and leaves the 112eff12 pin alone. The parser head and S5 then build on it. Answer here if you want it held or scoped differently.","body":{"stale_bases":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:2e19bdfbc9e886b05c992bed8ef572b1f37d17f2 stale; retired bases: git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:01e0dd696d953e8a4209c9e0a7461afc19c30b9a, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:043d587ef18b62fdeee6b57f8968de1567206697, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:070231aadcb17348891458bc2eedb5bbf529860c, git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:171f5f57d26458ff1e85c1f9e0a69fefb342bc9b and 19 more"}}